doc:sec-29ba-ss-3 a cof:Section ;
    cof:title "29BA(3) Commonwealth consent to conferral of functions etc. on" ;
    values:kind "subsection" ;
    values:originalText "(3) The Council cannot perform a duty or function, or exercise a power, under a State/Territory energy law unless the conferral of the function or power, or the imposition of the duty, is in  Chapter 1  Preliminary" ;
    cof:pageNumber "93"^^xsd:integer .

concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-29bb a cml:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "29BB How duty is imposed"@en ;
    cml:realizedBy doc:sec-29bb ;
    values:originalText "Application  (1) This section applies if a State/Territory energy law purports to impose a duty on the Council. Note: Section 29BC sets out when such a law imposes a duty on the Council. State or Territory legislative power sufficient to support duty  (2) The duty is taken not to be imposed by this Part (or any other law of the Commonwealth) to the extent to which:  (a) imposing the duty is within the legislative powers of the State or Territory concerned; and  (b) imposing the duty by the law of the State or Territory is consistent with the constitutional doctrines restricting the duties that may be imposed on the Council. Note: If this subsection applies, the duty will be taken to be imposed by force of the law of the State or Territory (the Commonwealth having consented under section 29BA to the imposition of the duty by that law). Commonwealth legislative power sufficient to support duty but State or Territory legislative powers are not  (3) If, to ensure the validity of the purported imposition of the duty, it is necessary that the duty be imposed by a law of the Commonwealth (rather than by the law of the State or Territory), the duty is taken to be imposed by this Part to the extent necessary to ensure that validity.  (4) If, because of subsection (3), this Part is taken to impose the duty, it is the intention of the Parliament to rely on all powers available to it under the Constitution to support the imposition of the duty by this Part.  Preliminary  Chapter 1 The National Competition Council  Part IIA  Section 29BC  Competition and Consumer Act 2010  Compilation date: 27/05/2026  (5) The duty is taken to be imposed by this Part in accordance with subsection (3) only to the extent to which imposing the duty:  (a) is within the legislative powers of the Commonwealth; and  (b) is consistent with the constitutional doctrines restricting the duties that may be imposed on the Council.  (6) Subsections (1) to (5) do not limit section 29BA. 29BC  When a State/Territory energy law imposes a duty  For the purposes of sections 29BA and 29BB, a State/Territory energy law imposes a duty on the Council if:  (a) the law confers a function or power on the Council; and  (b) the circumstances in which the function or power is conferred give rise to an obligation on the Council to perform the function or to exercise the power." ;
    skos:definition "Application  (1) This section applies if a State/Territory energy law purports to impose a duty on the Council. Note: Section 29BC sets out when such a law imposes a duty on the Council. State or Territory legislative power sufficient to support duty  (2) The duty is taken not to be imposed by this Part (or any other law of the Commonwealth) to the extent to which:  (a) imposing the duty is within the legislative powers of the State or Territory concerned; and  (b) imposing the duty by the law of the State or Territory is consistent with the constitutional doctrines restricting the duties that may be imposed on the Council. Note: If this subsection applies, the duty will be taken to be imposed by force of the law of the State or Territory (the Commonwealth having consented under section 29BA to the imposition of the duty by that law). Commonwealth legislative power sufficient to support duty but State or Territory legislative powers are not  (3) If, to ensure the validity of the purported imposition of the duty, it is necessary that the duty be imposed by a law of the Commonwealth (rather than by the law of the State or Territory), the duty is taken to be imposed by this Part to the extent necessary to ensure that validity.  (4) If, because of subsection (3), this Part is taken to impose the duty, it is the intention of the Parliament to rely on all powers available to it under the Constitution to support the imposition of the duty by this Part.  Preliminary  Chapter 1 The National Competition Council  Part IIA  Section 29BC  Competition and Consumer Act 2010  Compilation date: 27/05/2026  (5) The duty is taken to be imposed by this Part in accordance with subsection (3) only to the extent to which imposing the duty:  (a) is within the legislative powers of the Commonwealth; and  (b) is consistent with the constitutional doctrines restricting the duties that may be imposed on the Council.  (6) Subsections (1) to (5) do not limit section 29BA. 29BC  When a State/Terr"@en ;
    cml:integrityHash "e7ad522ea327e5ba95534ef8355038ef7f7b86328c7cf9175886f60464c40d28" ;
    cof:pageNumber "94"^^xsd:integer ;
    cml:hasPart concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-29bb-ss-1 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-29bb-ss-2 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-29bb-ss-3 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-29bb-ss-4 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-29bb-ss-5 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:competition-and-consumer-act-2010-sec-29bb-ss-6 ;
    cml:curationStatus cml:Proposed ;
    cml:schemaVersion "2" ;
    cml:proposedBy <https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml> ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf> ;
    dc:source "competition-and-consumer-act-2010.pdf, p.94" .
doc:sec-29bb a cof:Section ;
    cof:title "29BB How duty is imposed" ;
    values:kind "section" ;
    values:originalText "Application  (1) This section applies if a State/Territory energy law purports to impose a duty on the Council. Note: Section 29BC sets out when such a law imposes a duty on the Council. State or Territory legislative power sufficient to support duty  (2) The duty is taken not to be imposed by this Part (or any other law of the Commonwealth) to the extent to which:  (a) imposing the duty is within the legislative powers of the State or Territory concerned; and  (b) imposing the duty by the law of the State or Territory is consistent with the constitutional doctrines restricting the duties that may be imposed on the Council. Note: If this subsection applies, the duty will be taken to be imposed by force of the law of the State or Territory (the Commonwealth having consented under section 29BA to the imposition of the duty by that law). Commonwealth legislative power sufficient to support duty but State or Territory legislative powers are not  (3) If, to ensure the validity of the purported imposition of the duty, it is necessary that the duty be imposed by a law of the Commonwealth (rather than by the law of the State or Territory), the duty is taken to be imposed by this Part to the extent necessary to ensure that validity.  (4) If, because of subsection (3), this Part is taken to impose the duty, it is the intention of the Parliament to rely on all powers available to it under the Constitution to support the imposition of the duty by this Part.  Preliminary  Chapter 1 The National Competition Council  Part IIA  Section 29BC  Competition and Consumer Act 2010  Compilation date: 27/05/2026  (5) The duty is taken to be imposed by this Part in accordance with subsection (3) only to the extent to which imposing the duty:  (a) is within the legislative powers of the Commonwealth; and  (b) is consistent with the constitutional doctrines restricting the duties that may be imposed on the Council.  (6) Subsections (1) to (5) do not limit section 29BA. 29BC  When a State/Territory energy law imposes a duty  For the purposes of sections 29BA and 29BB, a State/Territory energy law imposes a duty on the Council if:  (a) the law confers a function or power on the Council; and  (b) the circumstances in which the function or power is conferred give rise to an obligation on the Council to perform the function or to exercise the power." ;
    cof:pageNumber "94"^^xsd:integer .

